        tagline: "Process fired heaters, reformers, cracking furnaces, flares and incinerators for refineries, petrochemicals and fertiliser plants, now diversifying into other process equipment and green hydrogen.",
        revenue: '₹819cr (FY26, consolidated)',
        products: [
            { name: 'Fired heaters, reformers and cracking furnaces', text: "The core business: designing, supplying, installing and commissioning the high-temperature furnaces at the heart of a refinery or petrochemical plant." },
            { name: 'Flares and incinerators', text: "Equipment to burn off waste gas safely. It books these orders directly in India and abroad, without the parent; on 29 September 2026 it won two domestic flare orders worth up to ₹100cr (about 11% of a year's sales), to be completed in 16 months." },
            { name: 'Non-heating projects', text: "Process plants and special fabricated equipment for offshore, steel, metals, minerals and material handling, which it wants to make 40% of revenue in 4–5 years." },
            { name: 'Green hydrogen (JNK-ChemDist JV)', text: "A joint venture making hydrogen as a by-product of turning ethanol into ethyl acetate, executing a ₹50cr project in Pune; it made 8.8% of Q1 FY27 revenue and a ₹3.6cr loss." },
        ],
        revenueText: "Revenue is project-based and now recognised as costs are incurred, a change from the earlier method. **The company does not split revenue by product or region.** It is strongly seasonal: **Q1 brings only 10–15% of the year's revenue and the second half 60–70%**. Its ₹6,000cr bid pipeline is roughly half export and half domestic, and about 60% of it is heating equipment.",
        customers: ["**Indian refiners and petrochemical companies**: BPCL (the Bina project, executed with JNK Global, is the anchor for FY27–28), IOCL, HPCL and Reliance.", "**Export refineries**, notably Dangote in Nigeria, where it supplied five heaters; export heater orders are won and guaranteed by the Korean parent, JNK Global, then subcontracted back.", "**Fertiliser and steel companies** for reformers and newer process work, such as jobs for Tata Steel and HPCL."],
        difficulty: { level: 'High', text: "A refinery furnace holds the fire at the centre of the plant, so owners and their process licensors only buy from pre-qualified suppliers. JNK does the thermal design and engineering itself but outsources most fabrication to workshops near the site." },
        moat: "Licensor pre-qualification and a long record keep new entrants out of fired heaters, where it wins 20–25% of bids. The same approvals cut both ways: an export order was cancelled when the licensor did not clear it in time, and for export heaters it depends on the parent's name and guarantees.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Refinery and petrochemical spending**: BPCL Bina is the biggest job, gave about 30% of FY26 revenue and is to be executed evenly over FY27 and FY28.", "**Export orders through JNK Global**: Dangote's second phase and a fertiliser reformer are expected, but have been promised before. The three-year cooperation agreement with the parent has not been formally renewed.", "**Diversification**: offshore, metals and material-handling work widens the market, but management expects to win only about 10% of bids there.", "**Guidance**: it now guides 20–25% revenue growth at a 12–14% margin, down from 25–30% and 14–15% earlier, though management says nothing changed.", "**Green hydrogen**: the JV is expected to break even by the end of FY27."],
        inputs: "**Fabricated steel parts and coils, alloy tubes, burners, refractories and structures**, mostly bought from sub-vendors because it outsources fabrication. Materials were 76% of sales in FY26.",
        costEffect: "**Partly protected.** Project prices are fixed, but it places orders with its sub-vendors at fixed prices soon after winning a job, so later swings in steel prices mostly land on the vendors. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 8 points (11% to 3%). **What happened:** India's alloy and stainless steel bar price index was flat (100 in Q1 FY24, 99 in Q1 FY27), yet the margin fell from 20.6% to 8.8%. The cause was the end of unusually profitable heater orders in FY24, a weaker project mix in FY25, and losses at the hydrogen JV in a seasonally small quarter, not steel.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'Heurtey Petrochem (Axens group)', note: 'Global fired-heater specialist and a direct rival for Indian and export heater orders.' }, { name: 'L&T and other large EPC firms', note: 'Stronger in big process plants; JNK targets mid-sized non-heating jobs they often skip.' }],
        position: "**One of the leading Indian suppliers of fired heaters, reformers and cracking furnaces**, with about ₹1,600cr of its March order book linked to its Korean parent.",
        money: "Screener shows **₹108cr of net cash** at March 2026. Cash rose from ₹29cr to ₹186cr in FY25, the year it listed. The business is asset-light, but operating cash flow has been negative in three of the last five years and unpaid bills were 154 days of sales in FY26; letting the parent post bank guarantees on export and joint jobs saves working capital. At the Chemdist hydrogen venture, 32% of shareholder votes went against its related-party deals, its own auditor was adverse on four points (including cash losses), and JNK guaranteed ₹25cr of its bank lines. Promoters hold **67.8%**, almost unchanged since listing.",
        watch: ["Formal renewal of the cooperation agreement with JNK Global.", "Dangote phase 2 and other export heater orders.", "Margin staying within the 12–14% guidance.", "Operating cash turning positive, and the size of the Chemdist related-party deals."],
        charts: industrialsHeavyCharts['JNK'],
