        revenueText: "**International operations were about 77% of Q1 FY27 revenue** (₹386cr of ₹501cr), up from about half a year earlier. In India, products brought in ₹72cr and projects only ₹33cr because water-scheme payments have stopped. Of the ₹5,270cr order book, ₹1,029cr is products and ₹4,241cr projects (including ₹530cr of O&M); international orders are ₹2,891cr.",
        mixes: [
            { caption: 'Revenue by business, Q1 FY27 (company)', parts: [{ label: 'International', share: 77 }, { label: 'India products', share: 15 }, { label: 'India projects', share: 7 }], note: 'About ₹10cr of other revenue is not split.' },
            { caption: 'Order book by type, June 2026 (company)', parts: [{ label: 'Projects incl. O&M', share: 80 }, { label: 'Products', share: 20 }] },
        ],
        customers: ["**State governments and water boards in India**, mostly for Jal Jeevan Mission rural water schemes (West Bengal, Madhya Pradesh), plus state irrigation departments.", "**Municipal water bodies in South Africa and Australia**, and oil and gas companies in Europe and the Middle East.", "**Power plants, nuclear projects, the Navy and heavy industry** for engineered pumps."],
        difficulty: { level: 'High', text: "Large engineered pumps need complex hydraulics, special metals and testing, and nuclear and naval work requires long qualification. Water projects are far easier to enter; there the skill is execution and getting paid." },
        moat: "Qualification for nuclear, naval and large irrigation pumps, and established European brands, make the product side sticky. The project side has no real moat: it eats capital and depends on state payments, as the Madhya Pradesh debarment notice showed.",
